---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { sitePath } from "../../lib/path.js";
import Example from "../../components/Example.astro";
import DocsMeta from "../../components/DocsMeta.astro";

const exampleCode = `<details class="collapse">
  <summary class="btn btn-primary mb-2">Toggle collapse</summary>
  <div class="card card-body">
    Some placeholder content for the collapse component. This panel is hidden by default but revealed when the user activates the relevant trigger. Find in page can still match it: the token is <strong>archaeopteryx</strong>.
  </div>
</details>`;

const horizontalCode = `<div style="min-height: 120px;">
  <details class="collapse collapse-horizontal">
    <summary class="btn btn-primary mb-2">Toggle width collapse</summary>
    <div class="card card-body" style="width: 300px;">
      This is some placeholder content for a horizontal collapse. It’s hidden by default and shown when triggered.
    </div>
  </details>
</div>`;

const multipleCode = `<div class="row">
  <div class="col">
    <details class="collapse">
      <summary class="btn btn-primary mb-2">Toggle first element</summary>
      <div class="card card-body">
        Some placeholder content for the first collapse component of this multi-collapse example. This panel is hidden by default but revealed when the user activates the relevant trigger.
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
  <div class="col">
    <details class="collapse">
      <summary class="btn btn-primary mb-2">Toggle second element</summary>
      <div class="card card-body">
        Some placeholder content for the second collapse component of this multi-collapse example. This panel is hidden by default but revealed when the user activates the relevant trigger.
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</div>`;

const untilFoundCode = `<p>Search this page for <strong>coelacanth</strong>.</p>
<div hidden="until-found">
  <div class="card card-body">
    Find in page revealed this region. The token is <strong>coelacanth</strong>.
  </div>
</div>`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Collapse · Bootstrap HTML"
  description="Native HTML collapse using details and summary, styled with Bootstrap 5.3.8."
>
  <h1>Collapse</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Show and hide a panel with <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> and
    <code>&lt;summary&gt;</code> — no Collapse plugin. The trigger and
    the panel must be parent and child.
  </p>

  <DocsMeta id="collapse" at="start" />

  <h2 class="h4">How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The widget is <code>details.collapse</code>. Add
      <code>open</code> to start expanded (replaces
      <code>.collapse.show</code>).
    </li>
    <li>
      The trigger is the <code>&lt;summary&gt;</code>, typically with
      <code>.btn</code>. It has to live inside the
      <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> — there is no
      <code>data-bs-target</code> / <code>href</code> pointing at a
      distant id.
    </li>
    <li>
      The browser exposes expanded/collapsed on the summary (replaces
      <code>aria-expanded</code> toggling from JavaScript). You can
      still add <code>aria-controls</code> if you give the panel an
      <code>id</code>, but it is not required.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>::details-content</code> is the panel, and height (or width
      with <code>.collapse-horizontal</code>) transitions on it. That
      transition also needs <code>interpolate-size: allow-keywords</code>,
      which only Chromium has, so Firefox and Safari snap the panel open.
      Do not put padding on <code>.collapse</code> itself — keep it on an
      inner wrapper such as <code>.card-body</code>, same as Bootstrap.
    </li>
    <li>
      The same <code>name</code> on several
      <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> elements makes the group exclusive
      (replaces <code>data-bs-parent</code>). See
      <a href={sitePath("/components/accordion")}>Accordion</a> for that pattern.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>&lt;summary&gt;</code> is a button-like control, not an
      <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Closed panels stay in the document, so Find in page can match
      them (try <strong>archaeopteryx</strong> in the first example).
      Do not put <code>hidden</code> or <code>display: none</code> on
      the inner wrapper. For a custom region that is not a
      <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> panel, use
      <code>hidden="until-found"</code> with padding on a nested
      child. Bootstrap’s reboot would otherwise force
      <code>display: none</code> and break that attribute.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Example
    title="Example"
    lead="Click the summary to show and hide the panel. .collapse hides content; [open] shows it; ::details-content handles the transition."
    code={exampleCode}
  >
    <div slot="alert" class="alert alert-warning" role="alert">
      Bootstrap’s example uses a link and a button that both target one
      distant <code>#collapseExample</code>. A
      <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> element has a single
      <code>&lt;summary&gt;</code>, so there is one trigger, and it
      must sit next to the panel.
    </div>
  </Example>

  <Example
    title="Horizontal"
    lead="Add .collapse-horizontal to transition width instead of height, and set a width on the immediate child of the panel."
    code={horizontalCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Multiple toggles and targets"
    lead="Each panel has its own summary. Independent details elements can be open at the same time."
    code={multipleCode}
  >
    <div slot="alert" class="alert alert-warning" role="alert">
      One control cannot open two panels, and two controls cannot share
      one panel — both need a
      <code>data-bs-target</code> selector and JavaScript. Native HTML
      also has no “Toggle both” button.
    </div>
  </Example>

  <Example
    title="Find in page"
    lead="hidden=&quot;until-found&quot; hides a custom region until Find in page matches it. Collapse panels themselves stay on details — do not put hidden on the card."
    code={untilFoundCode}
  />

  <DocsMeta id="collapse" at="end" />

  <h2 class="h4">Checkbox hack</h2>
  <p>
    A hidden checkbox plus a <code>&lt;label for&gt;</code> can toggle
    a sibling with <code>:checked</code>. That would let the trigger
    sit beside the panel (still not across the document), and several
    labels could share one id.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is not worth a second Collapse variant, mainly for WCAG:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.</strong> The control is exposed
      as a checkbox (“checked / not checked”), not as a disclosure
      (“expanded / collapsed”). <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> /
      <code>&lt;summary&gt;</code> already maps to that disclosure
      role. Fixing the checkbox with <code>role="button"</code> and
      live <code>aria-expanded</code> needs JavaScript, which is what
      the hack is meant to avoid.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>2.4.3 Focus Order / 2.4.7 Focus Visible.</strong> The
      real tab stop is the visually hidden input, so focus rings and
      AT cursor land off the visible button.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>1.3.1 Info and Relationships.</strong> A form control
      used as chrome is announced in forms mode and can be submitted
      with a surrounding form.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>2.1.1 Keyboard.</strong> A checkbox toggles with Space.
      A summary (and Bootstrap’s button trigger) also accepts Enter.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Keep remote or one-to-many toggles for JavaScript. For native
    collapse, <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> is the accessible control.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
